  "preamble": "Epenthetic [u] is used to adapt loanwords to languages that allow only open syllables. Japanese inserts /u/ after most word-final and preconsonantal consonants in borrowings (Christmas > kurisumasu), though it uses /o/ after t and d and /i/ after tʃ and dʒ. Swahili adds a final -u to Arabic loans ending in a labial consonant (kitāb > kitabu, qalam > kalamu).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Epenthesis, Paragoge, Loanword Adaptation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Japanese",
      "languageFamily": "Japonic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "English Christmas",
          "to": "クリスマス kurisumasu",
          "note": "/u/ is inserted after k and s to break clusters and after word-final s; Japanese /u/ is a weakly rounded [ɯᵝ]."
        },
        {
          "from": "English strike",
          "to": "ストライク sutoraiku",
          "note": "/u/ follows s and the final k, while /o/ is used after t."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Swahili",
      "languageFamily": "Bantu",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Arabic كِتَاب kitāb 'book'",
          "to": "kitabu",
          "note": "Final -u is added after the labial b; the first syllable ki- was reanalysed as the class 7 noun prefix."
        },
        {
          "from": "Arabic قَلَم qalam 'pen'",
          "to": "kalamu 'pen'",
          "note": "Final -u is added after the labial m."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
